<span data-scnav-slot></span><h1>Levels become each name's own lines, 34 studies, and the morning read</h1>
<p class="meta">27 September 2026 · F5 · branch hub/rulebook-stats-deep-20260925 off hub/release-20260923 @ 7c96a83 · built {{BUILT}} · daily bars through {{BARS}} (Friday's close) from the chart API · live reads captured {{CAPTURED}} from the deployed allocation tool and Hub · nothing pushed, nothing deployed, no database write · every number below is computed by the scripts named at the bottom, not typed</p>

<p class="lead">RSI 30 is not a level; it is a rare event, for almost everyone. It fired on about {{FIX30_C}} of days for the typical company over the last three years and {{FIX30_F}} for the typical fund. In the last twelve months {{NEVER1Y}} names never touched it at all, {{NEVER1Y_F}} of them funds. Each name's own line fires about as often as it says, and it moves more with trend than with the name.</p>

<div class="card tldr"><ul>
<li><b>RSI already divides out volatility, so the per-name lines sit close together.</b> Each company's own 10th-percentile RSI over three years sits from {{P10LINE_C}} for the middle 80% of companies, with a median of {{P10LINE_CM}}. For funds the median is {{P10LINE_FM}}. Bitcoin's is {{BTC_P10}}, SPY's {{SPY_P10L}}, the Dow fund's {{DIA_P10L}}. The fixed 30 sits well below all of them.</li>
<li><b>Trend state moves the line more than the name does.</b> Above its 200-day, a typical company's own 10th-percentile RSI is {{SHIFT_UP}}; below it, {{SHIFT_DN}}. Rulebook v3 draws RSI lines per trend state.</li>
<li><b>The per-name level that differs is the price depth, not the RSI.</b> At past pullback lows RSI was {{LOWRSI_M}} for the median company. The usual depth ran from {{LOWDEPTH}} across the middle 80% of companies: Walmart about 6%, Broadcom about 10%, Bitcoin about 19%, Bloom about 35%.</li>
<li><b>Alan's leader claim, measured three ways, is half right.</b> A 5% dip on a leader regained its high within 60 sessions {{L5.regained60}}% of the time; a 30% dip on a laggard only {{G30.regained60}}%. In plain returns the deep laggard bounced more. Scaled by each name's own volatility, both sit close to an ordinary day. No single piece is an edge, as Alan said.</li>
<li><b>Williams %R's fixed lines are not extremes.</b> Its −80 fired on {{WR80_C}} of company days and −20 on {{WR20_C}}. Its rank correlation with RSI is {{RSIWR}}: seat them as one witness.</li>
<li><b>The morning read is filled below from today's live reads.</b> The heat reads neutral. The tape is narrow: {{BR50}} of companies sit above their 50-day, their own {{BR50P}}th percentile over three years. The AI accelerators lead, and utilities, REITs and bond funds sit at their own RSI lows. MU reports Wednesday after the close.</li>
</ul></div>

<nav class="toc"><a href="#ch1">1 · levels → own lines</a><a href="#pullbacks">usual pullbacks</a><a href="#leaders">leader-relative</a><a href="#confluence">confluence (S6b)</a><a href="#v3text">v3 chapter 1 text</a><a href="#studies">2 · the {{SPECN}} studies</a><a href="#template">3 · the morning read</a><a href="#priority">4 · first ten</a><a href="#tags">news tags + operator inputs</a><a href="#wrong">what could be wrong</a></nav>

<div class="q">"We treat the RSI of the Dow exactly the same as the RSI of Bitcoin. A lot of indexes barely ever reach RSI 30; if we use that as a requirement for executions that fucks us. We need percentile-based, z-score-based, standard-deviation-based versions — probably all of them and a combination."<span>Alan, 25 September, about 5:30 PM ET</span></div>

<h2 id="ch1">1 · Rulebook v3, chapter 1 — every absolute level becomes the name's own line</h2>
<p>Rulebook v2 names four absolute levels: RSI 30 and 70, Williams %R −80 and −20, a distance to the 200-day (the package's ±10% reference lines, not the book's), and the board's two-usual-day move. Each gets three per-name forms here, and each form was tested the same way.</p>
<div class="card"><p><b>P, the percentile form.</b> Today's reading is placed among the name's own earlier readings, over three years or all history. The line "below its own 10th percentile" is the value that 10% of its earlier days sat under.</p>
<p><b>Z, the standard-score form.</b> Today's reading minus the name's own average, divided by its own spread. The line Z ≤ −1.28 is where a bell curve would put the 10th percentile. Drawn on a chart it is the "average minus k spreads" band, so the band version and the Z version are one rule, counted once.</p>
<p><b>σ, the sigma form, in the board's own unit.</b> The price move itself measured in the name's usual days (the 60-session spread of daily moves the board already shows). For RSI the move is the 14-session return. For the 200-day it is the distance to the average scaled by √(200/3), which is how far a random walk usually strays from its own 200-day. The line is again −1.28.</p>
<p class="note">Every line for a day is drawn only from the days before it. A day counts as "fired" when the reading is beyond the line. An episode is one run of firing days; a new one starts after five quiet sessions. Lines need 252 earlier readings. Windows are three years (756 sessions; about 1,095 days for Bitcoin, which trades every day) or all history. {{NAMES}} names: {{COMPANIES}} companies, {{FUNDS}} funds and BTCUSD. Missing for short history: {{MISSING}}.</p></div>

<h3>How often each line fired on days it had not seen</h3>
<p>Median share of days each rule fired over the last three years, with the middle half of names under it. A self-calibrating 10% line should read near 10%; the two-usual-day lines near 2.5%.</p>
{{CALIB_TABLE}}
<p>Four readings from this table.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>RSI.</b> The own-percentile, Z and all-history lines all fire near 10%. The fixed 30 fires on {{FIX30_C}} of company days and {{FIX30_F}} of fund days. Over all history it is {{FIX30_CF}} and {{FIX30_FF}}: funds reach 30 less often, but "never" is not true over three years.</li>
<li><b>Williams %R.</b> The fixed −80 fires on {{WR80_C}} of company days and −20 on {{WR20_C}} ({{WR20_F}} for funds). These lines mark ordinary days, not extremes. The own-percentile line fixes that. The Z line overshoots, because %R piles up at its ends.</li>
<li><b>Distance to the 200-day.</b> No form is well behaved. The fixed −10% fires on {{D200_C}} of company days, but the middle half of names runs {{D200_CI}}, and funds only {{D200_F}}. The three-year percentile fires {{D200_P3}}, ranging {{D200_P3I}}, because three years of a bull market pull the line up. The all-history percentile fires {{D200_PA}} lately and {{D200_PAF}} over all history. The σ form fires only {{D200_SIG}} ({{D200_SIGF}} over all history): the usual day widens during declines, which shrinks the scaled distance exactly when the price falls. This family measures trend, not stretch.</li>
<li><b>The daily move.</b> Two usual days down fired on {{SIG2_C}} of company days; the name's own 2.5th percentile fired {{SIGP_C}}. The board's sigma is already a per-name line, and it stays. Funds fall two usual days more often than they rise two (median gap {{SKEW_F}} points, against {{SKEW_C}} for companies): indexes carry negative skew, single names do not.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The thirteen, and Bitcoin</h3>
