<h3>The recommendation, one form per family</h3>
<div class="tw"><table class="rec"><thead><tr><th>family</th><th>form</th><th>window and line</th><th>why</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td data-l="family">RSI(14)</td><td data-l="form">P, the own percentile, drawn per trend state</td><td data-l="window">three years; the 10th and 90th percentile on days above the 200-day and on days below it; the fixed 30/70 shown for reference only</td><td data-l="why">Fires as often as it says. Needs no bell-curve assumption on a bounded, skewed dial. It is what S6b already uses. It handles the range shift (about {{SHIFT_UP}} against {{SHIFT_DN}}), which is bigger than any name effect.</td></tr>
<tr><td data-l="family">Williams %R(14)</td><td data-l="form">P, and seated with RSI as one witness</td><td data-l="window">three years, 10th / 90th</td><td data-l="why">The fixed lines fire on a fifth to a third of all days. Its rank correlation with RSI is {{RSIWR}} (middle half {{RSIWR_I}}), so counting both doubles one piece of evidence until study A5 measures their extremes.</td></tr>
<tr><td data-l="family">Distance to the 200-day</td><td data-l="form">P over all history, read as trend context, never as an execution gate</td><td data-l="window">all history, 10th / 90th; the pullback depth against the name's usual pullback (below) is the execution-facing number</td><td data-l="why">All-history calibrates over the long run ({{D200_PAF}}). The three-year line chases the trend, the fixed line means nothing across names, and the σ form under-fires.</td></tr>
<tr><td data-l="family">Daily move (sigma)</td><td data-l="form">σ, the board's usual day, unchanged</td><td data-l="window">±2 usual days, with the name's own 2.5th / 97.5th percentile shown beside it</td><td data-l="why">Already per name. It fires close to its own percentile line ({{SIG2_C}} against {{SIGP_C}}).</td></tr>
<tr><td data-l="family">The combination</td><td data-l="form">a stretch count, not an average</td><td data-l="window">how many independent families sit at their own line today: RSI (with %R as one), the pullback depth against its usual, the 200-day context</td><td data-l="why">Alan: "measure in a consolidated way." A count of independent witnesses stays legible and cannot be dominated by one family's scale. Whether a count of two beats a count of one is study D1's job, not an assumption.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h3 id="pullbacks">Each name's usual pullback: "to what RSI do we feel Walmart going down to is fair, versus Broadcom, versus Bitcoin and Nebius"</h3>
<p>An episode runs from a high to the close that regains it. It counts when the high was set at or above the 200-day and the lowest close sat at least 2.5 usual days below it. The table shows where those lows were: the median and the middle half.</p>
{{PB_TABLE}}
<ul>
<li><b>RSI at the low barely changes across names.</b> It sat from {{LOWRSI}} for the middle 80% of companies. Walmart's, Broadcom's and Bitcoin's pullbacks ended at about the same RSI.</li>
<li><b>The depth and the averages change a lot.</b> Walmart's usual pullback is about 6% and ends near its 200-day. Micron's is about 13.5% and ends well above it. Bitcoin's is about 19%, Bloom's about 35%. Nebius has only four measured pullbacks, all ending at an RSI between about 45 and 50, never near "oversold": that is what a leader's range shift looks like.</li>
<li><b>So the fair level per name is its usual depth, with the RSI line as the second witness.</b> Today, WMT sits about three times its usual depth below its high, AVGO nearly three times, GOOGL almost twice, MU at 0.8 of its usual depth two sessions before its report.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="leaders">The leader-relative form: "a 5% pullback on a leader is better than a 30% pullback in a laggard"</h3>
<p>Leader rank is the 120-session return minus SPY's, ranked among companies that day: the top third are leaders, the bottom third laggards. A trigger is the first close in an episode that reaches a depth below the high. "Its own usual depth" uses only pullbacks that had finished before the trigger. Everything after the trigger is counted, not predicted. {{TRIGN}} triggers in total.</p>
{{TRIG_TABLE}}
<ul>
<li><b>Getting back to the high.</b> The 5% leader dip regained its high within 60 sessions {{L5.regained60}}% of the time. The 30% laggard dip did so {{G30.regained60}}% of the time. On this measure Alan is right by a wide margin.</li>
<li><b>Plain return 60 sessions later.</b> The deep laggard dip ended higher {{G30.up60}}% of the time with a median of {{G30.med60}}%. The leader dip ended higher {{L5.up60}}% with {{L5.med60}}%. Deep laggards bounce harder in percent. They are also the rows most flattered by survivorship, because laggards that died are not in today's list.</li>
<li><b>Scaled by each name's own volatility, almost nothing separates.</b> The median 60-session return in usual-day units is {{L5.ret60u_med}} for the leader dip and {{G30.ret60u_med}} for the laggard dip, against {{BL.ret60u_med}} for any leader session. "A usual pullback on a leader" matches an ordinary leader day ({{OWN_L.up60}}% against {{BL.up60}}% higher). A usual pullback on a laggard is slightly worse than an ordinary laggard day ({{OWN_G.up60}}% against {{BG.up60}}%).</li>
</ul>
<p>Rulebook v3 therefore writes the leader-relative form as two numbers side by side: depth as a multiple of the name's own usual pullback, and leader rank. Neither is an entry by itself.</p>

<h3 id="confluence">Confluence, worked on S6b: every condition against the same condition's ordinary baseline</h3>
<p>S6b (branch hub/stats-s6-20260925 @ af2b2e6) is the model for how confluence is measured. Each state at the start of the 20 sessions before a report is compared with the same state's ordinary 20-session stretches. That separates "the report effect" from "the oversold-bounce effect".</p>
{{S6B_TABLE}}
<ul>
<li><b>The report effect holds in every state.</b> Every row, in every bucket, rose into the report more often than the same state's ordinary stretches, by about 1 to 5 points. The smallest lift, under 1 point, is for names more than 20% above their 200-day.</li>
<li><b>Conditions add a little; confluences do not multiply.</b> The best confluences reach 63–65% higher, against 59.8% for all reports. Their baselines rise too, so the lift stays around 3–5 points.</li>
<li><b>Two confluences have no ordinary baseline</b>, because "previous report beat" does not exist outside a report window. They cannot be read as lift.</li>
<li><b>This is exactly Alan's "no single piece is an edge."</b> Seven confluences were tried here. The next study must print how many combinations it tried, discover on 2003–2016 and confirm on 2017–2026.</li>
</ul>
<div class="card"><p><b>How S7 slots in.</b> The parallel Opus lane S7 is measuring entry triggers inside the pre-report window: own RSI percentile under 20/30/40, touches of the 20/50/100/200-day, drawdowns in usual days, prior horizontal levels, and a parallel channel's lower rail, alone and in pairs and triples. Its results enter the rulebook and the loop without duplication.</p>
<ol>
<li><b>Into rulebook v3 chapter 1</b> as "entry lines". Each trigger is a condition, stated in the per-name forms above: own percentile, usual days, usual pullback. It is never an absolute level.</li>
<li><b>Into the loop as study E1's second half and D1–D3's report-window cells.</b> The loop runs the same triggers on ordinary windows, so a trigger only counts if it beats its own state's ordinary baseline, S6b's rule.</li>
<li><b>Acceptance.</b> At least 200 trades in each half, the number of combinations tried on the page, and discover and confirm both shown. A combination that passes only one half is written down as dead.</li>
<li><b>Into the morning read</b> as one column on the run-up line: which of S7's surviving triggers is live today for each name reporting inside 20 sessions.</li>
</ol></div>

<h3 id="v3text">Rulebook v3, chapter 1 — the text, drafted</h3>
<div class="card"><ol>
<li><b>V3-1.1 · No absolute line on a bounded dial.</b> Every threshold on RSI, %R and any gauge state is PERCENTILE mode (U-3 made the default, no longer a toggle): the name's own three-year window, prior readings only, lines at the 10th and 90th percentile. The fixed Wilder 30/70 and Williams −80/−20 are printed as reference only and gate nothing.</li>
<li><b>V3-1.2 · Lines are drawn per trend state.</b> An RSI line is read from the days in the same state as today: above or below the 200-day until the S-4 trend verdict exists, then by X-2's regime. This is the I-6 range-shift law, cashed.</li>
<li><b>V3-1.3 · The usual pullback is a named per-name object.</b> Its depth in % and in usual days, and RSI and both averages at its lows, are recomputed nightly from finished bars. Today's drawdown is stated as a multiple of it ("× usual").</li>
<li><b>V3-1.4 · Leader-relative reading.</b> Wherever a pullback is cited, the leader rank (the 120-session return minus SPY's, ranked among companies) is cited beside it. Depth never appears without rank.</li>
<li><b>V3-1.5 · The daily move stays in usual days.</b> Two usual days is the board's line; the name's own 2.5th / 97.5th percentile is shown beside it.</li>
<li><b>V3-1.6 · The 200-day distance is context.</b> Its own all-history percentile is displayed and gates nothing.</li>
<li><b>V3-1.7 · Confluence is a count of independent witnesses.</b> RSI and %R count once until A5 clears them. Any combination claims only a lift over the same state's ordinary baseline, in both halves of history, with the number of combinations tried printed.</li>
<li><b>V3-1.8 · Nothing here is an entry.</b> Lines describe where a reading sits. Entries come from studies that beat their own baseline (S7, D1–D3), and each carries its verdict and its sample.</li>
</ol></div>

<hr>
<h2 id="studies">2 · The study catalogue — {{SPECN}} studies</h2>
<div class="q">"These are examples of studies. I hope you are creative and do more … All of this has a probability that is measured and has a theory — we're not reinventing anything … The main edge is name selection."<span>Alan, 25 September</span></div>
<p>Every spec follows the quant-loop plan's five parts: inputs, history, rule, verdict, display. Each adds its classical source, the data we have against the data we need, and how we could fool ourselves. No study predicts. Each describes how often something happened, with the count. Six are measured on this page as descriptive first cuts; S6/S6b exist; S7 is running.</p>
{{SPECS}}

