<h2 id="tags">Proposal: the operator inputs and the news tags</h2>
<p>Both are additive tables, written as one migration with its rollback and not applied: <code>supabase/migrations/20260927_operator_inputs_news_tags.sql</code>. The coordinator applies it. Until then the morning read keeps its inputs on the page only.</p>
<div class="card"><p><b>operator_inputs is append-only.</b> A new decision is a new row, so the history of what Alan believed, and when, is never overwritten. Its fields are the name, the state, the reason in Alan's words, set_at, and review_on. A kind field separates states, plans and change requests, and supersedes links a row to the one it replaces. The K1 table operator_preferences keeps one row per key, which erases history, so it cannot hold a dated regime.</p>
<p><b>news_event_tags adds one row per headline × ticker × tag.</b> It holds the counterparty, the stance the headline itself states (not a model's tone), first_seen_utc, the first session that could trade on it, a story cluster id, who tagged it (a rule version or Alan), and whether Alan confirmed it.</p>
<p><b>The tagging pipeline is rules first and uses no paid model.</b></p>
<ol>
<li><b>Match.</b> Headline and summary patterns per tag: customer_concentration, hyperscaler_deal_named_provider, capex_guidance, export_control, supply_constraint, guidance_change, index_change, other. The entity lists are our universe's tickers and a counterparty list: the hyperscalers MSFT, AMZN, GOOGL, META and ORCL, plus the named neoclouds.</li>
<li><b>Cluster.</b> The same tickers and tag within 48 hours, with similar titles, form one story.</li>
<li><b>Clock.</b> Before 9:30 ET trades the same session; after 16:00 trades the next.</li>
<li><b>Confirm.</b> The morning read shows yesterday's tagged events on leaders, and Alan confirms or rejects each one.</li>
<li><b>Count.</b> Study H4 reads confirmed rows only, and the page says how many were rejected.</li>
</ol>
<p class="note">The Loughran–McDonald headline lexicon already in the Hub scores tone. Tone is kept as a column, never as the tag.</p></div>

<hr>
<h2 id="wrong">Where each number comes from, and what could be wrong</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Bars.</b> The chart API's finished daily bars (GET /candles?tf=D&amp;limit=6000, origin scintillahub.ai), split-adjusted as served, fetched once into a scratch folder on 27 September. The newest bar is Friday {{BARS}}. BTCUSD comes from FMP through the same API and trades every calendar day, so its RSI(14) spans 14 calendar days.</li>
<li><b>Live reads.</b> Headless Chromium read the deployed allocation tool and Hub at {{CAPTURED}}. The raw text and a screenshot of each sit in evidence/. No database was read directly, and no key was used by this session. The pages used their own public read path.</li>
<li><b>Survivors only.</b> Every historical count uses today's 364 names. The laggard and breadth numbers are flattered the most.</li>
<li><b>Hindsight in the pullback bands.</b> Lows are chosen after the fact. That is why the leader table uses live-knowable triggers instead.</li>
<li><b>Overlap and clustering.</b> One selloff triggers many names at once; the "months" column shows how many distinct months the triggers come from. No intervals are computed here. That is the loop's job, with block bootstraps.</li>
<li><b>My thresholds.</b> 2.5 usual days for a pullback, 250 sessions to give up, five quiet sessions between episodes, the top and bottom thirds for leaders and laggards, and the +200% / −75% splice guard. Each is stated, and each changes the counts.</li>
<li><b>The splice guard.</b> A real one-day move of +200% would be cut. None was found on inspection of the 60 moves over +100% or under −60%, but the rule is blunt. Six histories were cut: {{CUTS}}.</li>
<li><b>The σ form for the 200-day uses a random-walk scale.</b> It under-fires badly. That is reported as a finding, not tuned away.</li>
<li><b>Cohort labels are today's taxonomy.</b> The AI-power branch is mostly regulated utilities, which is why the AI core is shown separately.</li>
<li><b>The heat is read as served.</b> Several of its sources were flagged stale or fallback at capture; the page lists them in line 1.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What I did not do</h2>
<ul>
<li>Nothing pushed, deployed, merged or scheduled. No database read or write, and no migration applied. The migration is a file.</li>
<li>No study that predicts. The leader and sigma "what followed" blocks are counts after live-knowable triggers, with no intervals.</li>
<li>I did not duplicate S7. Its in-window entry triggers are specified to slot into chapter 1, the loop and line 6.</li>
<li>I did not build the channel port (S7 does), the Trendoscope Phase 2 (it needs Alan's decisions), or the news tagger (proposal only).</li>
<li>I did not touch the Indicator Lab, the prototypes, the Hub's rooms or the allocation tool; all were read only. The parallel-channel and Trendoscope Pine files were read, not edited.</li>
<li>A prior run of this lane on 25 September left two uncommitted files here (levels-map.mjs and its output). They are untouched, not committed, and copied to the run folder. This page's scripts supersede them.</li>
</ul>

